### A6  S15: the index slip in the Mobius-correlated 2-adic point
A6 explorer's target [mu(k+1)=1], k=0..31: 10000100010001100000110001000000 -> x = 890931253 mod 2^32 (explorer: 890931253)
    corrected target (parity(x)=0; [mu(k)=1] for k=1..31): 01000010001000110000011000100000 -> x = 1781862506 mod 2^32
    With the explorer's indexing f(T^n x) = parity(T^n x) = [mu(n+1)=1], so (1/N) sum mu(n) f(T^n x) = (1/N) sum mu(n)[mu(n+1)=1],
    NOT (1/N)#{mu(n)=1}.  Demonstration on 2000-bit truncations, N = 1999:
    explorer's point: (1/N) sum mu(n) parity(T^n x) = -0.002001;  corrected point: +0.305153;  #{mu(n)=1}/N = 0.305153;  3/pi^2 = 0.303964
    (Trivial repair, and the conclusion 'disjointness is FALSE on Z_2' survives with the corrected point; the note's
    formula (1/N)#{mu(n)=1} was wrong for the point as defined.  The correlation with the explorer's point is
    (1/2) sum mu(n) mu(n+1)^2 + (1/2) sum mu(n) mu(n+1), whose second term is the 2-point Chowla sum: OPEN for natural density.)
    A 2-adic point with parity vector [mu=1] is not in Q: rational x have eventually periodic parity vectors (Lagarias 1985, CITED)
    and [mu=1] is not eventually periodic (every class r mod P contains infinitely many n with mu(n)=1 and with mu(n)=0).

